# Morrow v0.3 env template (plan §10 Env/secrets). NEVER commit real secrets.
# Quickstart: cp .env.example .env && ./deploy/init-env.sh

# --- App ---
# Canonical public URL of this instance (used for VAPID subject, absolute
# links, and external integrations). Web Push click URLs and CalDAV hrefs are
# relative, so other origins can still serve the app; APP_URL is the one used
# for generated links.
#
# Bundled Caddy (default):
#   SITE_ADDRESS=:80 with APP_URL=http://localhost       (plain HTTP, local)
#   SITE_ADDRESS=localhost with APP_URL=https://localhost (Caddy internal CA)
#   SITE_ADDRESS=morrow.example.com with APP_URL=https://morrow.example.com
# Tailscale Serve (compose.tailscale.yaml):
#   APP_URL=https://<machine>.<tailnet>.ts.net:8443
#   TRUST_PROXY=172.22.0.0/16
#   (the app runs in Docker, so the peer is the `edge` gateway, not loopback;
#    confirm the subnet with `docker network inspect morrow_edge`)
# External TLS proxy (compose.external.yaml):
#   APP_URL=https://morrow.example.com
#   TRUST_PROXY=172.22.0.0/16
#   (again the app sees the `edge` gateway when it runs in Docker; use
#    127.0.0.1/32 only when the app itself runs on the host)
# The Caddy pair must match; do not mix http://localhost with a hostname.
APP_URL=http://localhost
# Site address for the bundled Caddy only. A bare :80 is plain HTTP
# (zero-config local). A hostname enables automatic HTTPS (localhost = internal
# CA, real domain = public TLS). Must match APP_URL's origin. The
# compose.tailscale.yaml, compose.external.yaml, and compose.loopback.yaml
# overlays clear this for the app because APP_URL names a different front door;
# in loopback mode Caddy itself still reads the value from .env.
SITE_ADDRESS=:80
# SQLite file inside the app-data volume.
DATABASE_PATH=/data/morrow.db
# Nightly/manual backup output dir (inside app-data volume or host bind).
BACKUP_DIR=/data/backups
# Dir for 0600 secret files (mounted from app-data or host; see deploy/init-env.sh).
SECRETS_DIR=/data/secrets
# Port the app listens on inside the container (Caddy proxies to app:3000).
PORT=3000
MCP_PORT=3001
# Keep containers on UTC; per-user timezones live in settings (plan §3).
TZ=UTC
# Exact proxy CIDR(s) only, comma-separated for a chain (e.g.
# "172.22.0.0/16,127.0.0.1/32"). Never true/*; omit for direct access.
# Only set this when a proxy forwards X-Forwarded-For/Proto to the app.
#   app in Docker (the default): use the `edge` network subnet for bundled
#     Caddy, a proxy container on `edge`, or a host-level proxy publishing a
#     Docker port (the app sees the edge gateway, not loopback)
#   app on the host / host networking: 127.0.0.1/32 for a local proxy
# Find the subnet with:
#   docker network inspect morrow_edge --format '{{range .IPAM.Config}}{{.Subnet}}{{end}}'
# TRUST_PROXY=

# --- Compose front-door overlays ---
# The default `docker compose up -d --build` runs bundled Caddy.
# For Tailscale Serve or an external TLS proxy, use an overlay:
#   docker compose -f compose.yaml -f compose.tailscale.yaml up -d --build app
#   docker compose -f compose.yaml -f compose.external.yaml up -d --build app
# Tailscale/external disable Caddy; compose.loopback.yaml keeps Caddy for other
# sites. All three publish the app on the loopback port below.
# Keep the bind on 127.0.0.1 unless a proxy outside this host needs it; if you
# set 0.0.0.0, firewall the port.
# MORROW_LOOPBACK_PORT=3300
# MORROW_PROXY_BIND=127.0.0.1

# --- Auth/session (generated 0600 by deploy/init-env.sh; leave blank here) ---
# 256-bit deployment secret (0600). v0.1 session tokens are independent random
# values hashed in SQLite, so changing this alone does not revoke sessions.
SESSION_SECRET=
# First-run gate. open (default): the first visitor creates the owner account.
# token: POST /api/setup also requires SETUP_TOKEN — use this before exposing
# the app to the public internet. The app warns at boot if open mode is used
# with a public APP_URL.
SETUP_MODE=open
# One-time setup token, used only when SETUP_MODE=token. Deleted after the
# first user when stored as a file; the .env copy is inert after setup.
SETUP_TOKEN=
# Optional gate for GET /readyz (header X-Readyz-Token). Prefer the 0600
# SECRETS_DIR/readyz_token file; /readyz is open when neither is set.
# READYZ_TOKEN=

# --- Backups (see docs/SECURITY.md) ---
# Upload/restore cap in MiB (1-128). The default is 25. Raise it for a
# long-lived workspace whose core JSON backup legitimately exceeds 25 MiB.
BACKUP_MAX_MB=25
# Optional HMAC-SHA256 key for backup tamper resistance. Keep it outside
# BACKUP_DIR; prefer SECRETS_DIR/backup_hmac_key (0600) or set it here.
# BACKUP_HMAC_KEY=
# v0.2 age encryption: set an age recipient to write real `.json.age` exports.
# Generate a key pair with `age-keygen`; keep the secret key outside the repo.
# BACKUP_AGE_RECIPIENT=age1...
# Decryption key for uploads/restores. Prefer SECRETS_DIR/backup_age_identity
# (0600) or set it here.
# BACKUP_AGE_IDENTITY=AGE-SECRET-KEY-1...

# --- Retention ---
# The scheduler prunes activity/audit rows older than this many days once per
# day. 90 keeps a quarter of undo/history; 365 keeps a year; 0 disables
# pruning. JSON backups independently cap activity at the newest 5,000 rows so
# backups stay below the restore upload cap.
ACTIVITY_RETENTION_DAYS=90

# --- Integrations (v0.2) ---
# Task attachment bytes. Defaults next to DATABASE_PATH (/data/attachments).
# ATTACHMENTS_DIR=/data/attachments
# ntfy bearer token; never commit it. Topic/url are managed in Settings.
# NTFY_TOKEN=
# Allow an http:// and/or private-host ntfy URL for a trusted LAN instance
# (HTTPS and public hosts remain the default).
# NTFY_ALLOW_HTTP=0
# NTFY_ALLOW_PRIVATE=0
# Deprecated CalDAV password fallback for headless clients. Prefer a per-user
# kind=caldav token from Settings -> Integrations. When set, this env value maps
# only to the workspace owner (never an arbitrary first account).
# CALDAV_TOKEN=
# Comma-separated sender addresses accepted by POST /api/inbound/email.
# EMAIL_ALLOWED_FROM=

# --- OpenCode sidecar (agent network private; egress only for providers) ---
OPENCODE_URL=http://opencode:4096
# AI-off: leave OPENCODE_URL blank and set MCP_ENABLED=false. Keep
# OPENCODE_PASSWORD/REMINDERS_MCP_TOKEN non-empty (compose interpolates them
# even when the opencode service is not started). Then start only `caddy app`.
# Reminders/push/backup do not need the sidecar.
# Basic-auth password for `opencode serve` (generated 0600).
# MORROW_OPENCODE_PASSWORD is the canonical value. It wins over
# OPENCODE_PASSWORD so a host environment variable exported for another tool
# cannot make the app and sidecar disagree. Existing deployments may keep using
# OPENCODE_PASSWORD; init-env.sh copies it into the new name.
MORROW_OPENCODE_PASSWORD=
OPENCODE_PASSWORD=
# Bearer token the sidecar uses for app MCP http://app:3001/mcp (generated 0600,
# stored hashed server-side; see docs/MCP.md).
REMINDERS_MCP_TOKEN=
# MCP listener :3001 is agent-network only. Kill switch + Host/Origin allowlist.
MCP_ENABLED=true
MCP_ALLOWED_HOSTS=app,localhost,127.0.0.1,::1,172.30.250.10
# Absolute workspace path inside the OpenCode container (config + prompt mount).
OPENCODE_WORKSPACE=/workspace

# --- Web Push / VAPID (generate with `npx web-push generate-vapid-keys`) ---
VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=
VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=
# Real mailto: or https: contact. Apple rejects localhost (plan §7).
VAPID_SUBJECT=mailto:admin@example.com

# --- Provider credentials (M4.1) ---
# Morrow renders the OpenCode overlay here. Compose must map this DIRECTORY
# into the sidecar's GLOBAL config dir (e.g. /home/opencode/.config/opencode/)
# so its config watcher hot-reloads provider changes without a restart. Mount a
# directory, not a single file: atomic renames must stay visible to the sidecar.
# The sidecar must also see SECRETS_DIR/providers at the SAME absolute path,
# because the overlay references keys as {file:/data/secrets/providers/<id>.key}.
PROVIDER_OVERLAY_PATH=/data/opencode-providers/global.json
# Kill switch for provider credential writes. Reads/tests keep working.
PROVIDERS_ENABLED=true
# Allow http:// + private/loopback provider base URLs for Ollama / LM Studio.
# Cloud metadata endpoints stay blocked even when true. Default false.
ALLOW_PRIVATE_PROVIDER_URLS=false
# Explicit opt-in for a certified self-hosted push relay. Private/loopback/
# link-local targets remain blocked. Leave unset for the provider allowlist.
# PUSH_ALLOW_ANY_PUBLIC_ENDPOINT=1
# Replace the built-in provider host list (comma-separated). Private/loopback
# checks still apply. Only set this for a relay you control.
# PUSH_ENDPOINT_ALLOWLIST=

# --- Provider overlay mount contract (compose.yaml) ---
# compose.yaml pins the container paths used by the shared volumes:
#   PROVIDER_OVERLAY_PATH=/data/opencode-providers/global.json  (app writes)
#   SECRETS_DIR=/data/secrets                                   (app writes)
# The app and sidecar mount the secrets path identically. `opencode-providers`
# is mounted inside the sidecar's global config directory and loaded through
# OPENCODE_CONFIG; `opencode-secrets` supplies the
# {file:/data/secrets/providers/<id>.key} targets read by OpenCode. Changing a
# path here does not move a named volume: update compose.yaml and this comment
# together, or point both services at the same host bind directory.
#
# PROVIDERS_ENABLED=false is a write kill switch. Provider reads and connection
# tests keep working. ALLOW_PRIVATE_PROVIDER_URLS=true permits http:// Ollama /
# LM Studio endpoints; cloud metadata endpoints stay blocked. Both flags are
# read by the app, not the sidecar.
#
# Host-side equivalents are created by ./deploy/init-env.sh under ./data:
# 0700 dirs, 0600 key files, and a 0644 empty overlay placeholder. Re-run it
# after changing DATA paths in .env.

# --- Compose isolation: MCP bind + sidecar mounts (security) ---
# MCP (:3001) is agent-network only. compose.yaml assigns the app a fixed
# address on the internal `agent` network and sets MCP_HOST to it, so the
# listener never binds 0.0.0.0 (which would also answer on the edge/Caddy
# network). The sidecar also joins a separate non-internal `egress` network for
# cloud provider inference; the app and Caddy are not on it, and 4096/3001 are
# never published. When MCP_HOST is unset outside compose, the server falls
# back to 127.0.0.1 for local development. Never set MCP_HOST=0.0.0.0 in
# compose.
# Override the subnet and address TOGETHER if 172.30.250.0/24 collides with a
# host, VPN, or another Docker network:
# MORROW_AGENT_SUBNET=172.30.250.0/24
# MORROW_AGENT_IP=172.30.250.10

# Front-door (`edge`) subnet used by Caddy, proxy containers, and the Docker
# gateway for published app ports. When the app runs in Docker, TRUST_PROXY
# must include this subnet (see the App section above). Change both together
# if 172.22.0.0/16 collides with the host or another network.
# MORROW_EDGE_SUBNET=172.22.0.0/16

# --- Dev compose host port (compose.dev.yaml) ---
# `pnpm dev` publishes the app container's :3000 here. The default avoids the
# common local conflict with another tool already on :3000. If you browse the
# app on this port, set APP_URL to the same origin (e.g.
# http://localhost:3100) so absolute links and PWA scope match.
# MORROW_DEV_PORT=3100
#
# The sidecar no longer receives the whole SECRETS_DIR. Its mount contract is:
#   app:      opencode-secrets -> /data/secrets                    (rw, full dir)
#   opencode: opencode-secrets (volume subpath providers)
#             -> /data/secrets/providers                            (ro)
#   app:      opencode-providers -> /data/opencode-providers        (rw)
#   opencode: opencode-providers
#             -> /home/opencode/.config/opencode/providers          (ro)
# Only SECRETS_DIR/providers is visible to the sidecar; VAPID, backup HMAC,
# MCP, setup, session, and readyz secrets stay app-only. Keep provider keys at
# SECRETS_DIR/providers so {file:/data/secrets/providers/<id>.key} resolves
# unchanged. Do not widen the sidecar to /data/secrets: that would restore
# read access to every other secret.

